From 07c022e97a9a30075a133aac67c75741b8396b07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 08:48:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Split app.py into a caddy_manager package; misc
 simplifications

app.py had grown to ~900 lines mixing config storage, auth, Caddyfile
string building/parsing, block-metadata management, and every route.
Split into a package following the standard Flask 'application factory +
blueprints' layout, with templates/ and static/ left exactly where they
are (pointed at explicitly from the factory) so nothing on disk needs to
move:

- caddy_manager/configstore.py  - config.json load/save/is_configured/get_caddy_dir
- caddy_manager/colors.py       - TYPE_COLORS/type_color (unchanged)
- caddy_manager/caddyfile.py    - pure Caddyfile string building & parsing
                                  (split_target/join_target, normalize/
                                  render/extract helpers, slugify) -- no
                                  file I/O, easy to reason about in isolation
- caddy_manager/blocks.py       - the filesystem/metadata layer (safe_path,
                                  read/write_metadata, list_blocks,
                                  unique_filename, rename-on-address-change)
                                  plus a new build_block_from_form() helper
                                  (see below)
- caddy_manager/auth.py         - login_required + an 'auth' blueprint
                                  (setup/login/logout)
- caddy_manager/views.py        - a 'main' blueprint with every other route
- caddy_manager/__init__.py     - create_app() factory: wires up the
                                  secret-key handling, registers both
                                  blueprints and the type_color Jinja global
- app.py                        - now just creates the app via the factory
                                  and runs the dev server (9 lines)

Blueprints mean route endpoint names now carry a prefix (e.g. 'site_blocks'
-> 'main.site_blocks', 'login' -> 'auth.login'); every url_for() reference
across the templates has been updated to match.

Simplification: new_block() and edit_block() each had their own ~55-line
copy of the same four-block-type if/elif chain (parse fields, validate,
render Caddyfile content, build the metadata dict). Both now call a single
blocks.build_block_from_form(block_type, form), removing that duplication
-- every field a saved block needs is always fully resubmitted on every
save, so there was never actually a need for edit_block to separately
merge/preserve anything from the block's previous metadata. Minor
side-effect: the redirect_code default (falls back to '301' if somehow
blank) is now applied consistently in both new and edit, instead of only
in new_block.

Bug fix (found while touching this code, confirmed present in the current
code too, not introduced by this change): app.secret_key started out
unset (None) until the first before_request handler ran, but Flask opens
the session before before_request fires -- so the very first request an
app instance ever handles (in practice, whichever request happens to hit
the process first) got a NullSession, and setup's flash() call crashed
with a 500. create_app() now seeds app.secret_key with a random value
immediately, which also let the old app.config['_TEMP_SECRET'] plumbing
be removed as redundant.

Verified with the Flask test client (TESTING + PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS on, so
nothing is silently swallowed): full setup -> login -> create one of each
block type -> edit (including a rename-triggering site-address change) ->
preview -> toggle -> delete -> settings walk-through, plus a pyflakes pass
across every new module (no unused imports/names), plus a jsdom run
confirming the front-end JS still targets the right element ids in the
newly-rendered templates.
